      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] November 1974. Cleveland, Ohio. [PAUSE 1.5s] A slate-gray wind off Lake Erie rattled the corrugated steel walls of Cuyahoga Stamping and Press, carrying the smell of wet coal and sulfur through the loading dock. Inside Bay 4, the air was thick with atomized cutting oil and the rhythmic, chest-thumping [EMPHASIS: boom-hiss, boom-hiss] of twelve four-hundred-ton Bliss presses. \n\nThomas Halvorsen stood just inside the yellow safety line, his breath misting in the draft. At fifty-two, Thomas had spent twenty-five years as an independent industrial safety trainer, walking into plants that other consultants had given up on. He wore steel-toed boots scuffed down to the leather, a tweed coat dusted with chalk, and carried a battered aluminum clipboard under his arm. [BEAT] His routine never changed. Before he read a single binder, he listened. A healthy factory floor had a steady, predictable cadence. When machines were hurried or operators were tense, the rhythm stuttered. \n\nCuyahoga Stamping was supposed to be a routine checkup. On paper, it was a miracle. [SLOW DOWN] Hanging thirty feet above the center aisle, suspended from steel cables, was a freshly painted wooden banner twenty feet wide: [EMPHASIS: ONE HUNDRED AND FORTY-TWO DAYS WITHOUT A LOST-TIME INJURY.] \n\nYet Thomas had been summoned by the plant's insurance underwriter with an ultimatum. Two months earlier, a sister facility in Toledo had lost a press operator's hand, and the underwriter's actuaries had noticed something strange in Cuyahoga's numbers: zero lost-time accidents, yet a quiet, unexplained spike in worker turnover and equipment repairs. If Thomas could not find the rot behind the numbers within thirty days, the policy would be canceled, and three hundred men and women would be out of work before Christmas. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Thomas spent his first afternoon doing what every textbook told him to do. He walked the aisles with a checklist. He measured the light curtains on Press 7. He tested the two-hand palm buttons on Press 11. He checked the decibel levels, the ear protection bins, the oil-drip pans. Everything was [EMPHASIS: spotless]. \n\nAt four o'clock, hecornered the shift foreman, Frank Kowalski, beside the time clock. Frank was a broad-shouldered man with iron-gray hair and a thermos tucked under his arm like a football. \n\n[LEAN IN] \"Frank,\" Thomas said, tapping his clipboard. \"Your guards are tight. Your floor is clean. So why is your turnover up eighteen percent since July?\" \n\nFrank didn't blink. He pointed a thick thumb up at the wooden banner overhead. \"You see that sign, Halvorsen? [EMPHASIS: The cleanest floor in the valley.] People leave because the work is loud and the winters are long. Don't go looking for ghosts where there aren't any.\" \n\n\"I'm not looking for ghosts,\" Thomas replied. \"I'm looking for what happens when a man gets nicked and doesn't want to be the one to climb that ladder and paint a zero.\" \n\nFrank's jaw tightened. \"Nobody hides blood on my shift. You're a trainer, not a detective. Sign the sheet and catch your flight.\" \n\n[LOWER VOICE] Thomas didn't sign. As he walked back toward the exit, he passed a twenty-year-old press helper named Eddie Morales standing behind a stack of galvanized blanks. Eddie was furiously wrapping a grease-stained shop rag around his left thumb. Bright red soaked through the gray cotton. When Eddie saw Thomas watching, he jammed his hand deep into his coveralls, forced a stiff nod, and walked straight past the infirmary door toward the parking lot. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] That night, at eleven-thirty, Thomas sat on the edge of a sagging mattress in a motel off Euclid Avenue. Rain ticked against the venetian blinds. Beside him sat a cold Styrofoam cup of coffee and three months of OSHA logs that showed nothing\u2014[EMPHASIS: absolute, unbroken perfection.] \n\nFor the first time in a decade, Thomas wondered if he was losing his edge. Maybe Frank was right. Maybe he had spent so long hunting for disasters that he no longer knew how to recognize a good plant when he saw one. He rubbed his eyes, reached for his pen to draft a clean report, and froze. \n\nA soft knock sounded at the door. By the time Thomas opened it, the hallway was empty, save for a manila folder lying on the welcome mat. [STILLNESS] \n\nInside were handwritten pages torn from a spiral notebook\u2014the night-shift nurse's private log, kept off the official books. Thomas read them under the yellow desk lamp, his stomach sinking with every line. [SPEED UP] *August 4: deep laceration, index finger, refused stitches, wrapped in tape.* *September 19: pinched nail bed, Press 3, sent back to line.* *October 12: near-miss, die slipped, operator jumped clear, told not to file.* [SLOW DOWN] \n\nTwenty-seven entries in four months. [EMPHASIS: Twenty-seven invisible warnings.] Not one of them had touched the official ledger, because nobody on Bay 4 wanted to be the person who broke the streak and cost the shift their quarterly steak dinner. The banner wasn't measuring safety. It was manufacturing silence. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At six forty-five the next morning, the day shift was gathered around the time clock, waiting for the buzzer. Thomas didn't bring his clipboard. Instead, he wheeled a twelve-foot rolling ladder into the center of Bay 4, climbed to the top rung, and unhooked the left steel cable holding the wooden banner. \n\n[EMPHASIS: CRASH.] \n\nTwenty feet of painted pine slammed onto the concrete floor, splintering at the corner. Every press in the bay sat idle. Three dozen workers stared in dead silence. \n\nFrank Kowalski came charging down the aisle, his face purple. \"Have you lost your mind, Halvorsen? That's company property!\" \n\nThomas climbed down slowly, stepped over the broken wood, and faced the floor. [WARM SMILE] \"Gentlemen,\" he said, his voice carrying clearly over the hum of the overhead heaters. \"That sign told you that if you got hurt, you failed. So you stopped telling the truth. And when you stop telling the truth about small things, [EMPHASIS: the big things wait in the dark.]\" \n\nFrank opened his mouth to argue, then caught sight of Eddie Morales standing in the back row with a fresh white bandage on his thumb. Frank closed his mouth. \n\nThomas reached into his coat pocket and pulled out a crisp twenty-five-dollar voucher for the diner down the street. \"Starting today, there are no more streak boards. Instead, every morning at seven, one person on this floor is going to tell us about the worst near-miss they saw yesterday. The person who brings us the scariest close call\u2014the one that could have cost a hand\u2014wins this voucher. [BEAT] Who wants to go first?\" \n\n[SCENE SHIFT] For three days, nobody spoke. They thought it was a trap. On the fourth morning, Eddie Morales slowly raised his bandaged hand. He admitted that on Press 9, the foot-pedal guard had a sticking spring, so he'd been wedging it open with a wooden shim to make his quota. \n\nMaintenance pulled Press 9 offline that afternoon. When they opened the housing, the main clutch pin was cracked halfway through. Another two hundred cycles, and the four-hundred-ton ram would have dropped without warning. [PAUSE 2s] \n\nOver the next six months, reported near-misses at Cuyahoga Stamping [EMPHASIS: tripled.] On paper, it looked like the plant had become three times more dangerous. In reality, actual lost-time injuries dropped to zero\u2014and stayed there for four years. \n\n[SLOW DOWN] When Thomas Halvorsen finally drove out of Cleveland in the spring rain, the wooden banner was still gone. In its place hung a simple chalkboard, covered in messy, honest handwriting. Because Thomas had proved what every leader eventually learns: [LOWER VOICE] the safest room in the world isn't the one where nothing ever goes wrong. It's the one where people are [EMPHASIS: allowed to be wrong out loud.] [PAUSE 2s]",
