      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] In March of 1991, Henri Delacroix stood at the edge of a twelve-acre Chardonnay parcel in Meursault, Burgundy, watching his neighbors finish the winter pruning. [PAUSE 1.5s] The air smelled of damp limestone and woodsmoke from the burn barrels where clipped vine canes crackled into ash. Across the valley, every vineyard looked like a military parade ground\u2014[EMPHASIS: neat, low, and mercilessly uniform]. Each vine had been cut back to two short spurs, tied down to the wire, and cleared of every stray shoot. [BEAT] Henri\u2019s parcel, by contrast, looked like a ruin. Long, tangled canes from the previous autumn still arched three feet into the gray sky, swaying in the cold wind off the C\u00f4te d\u2019Or. Every morning at six, Henri walked those rows in his waxed canvas coat, a pair of Felco secateurs resting untouched in his leather holster, checking the soil thermometer and the barometer nailed to the stone shed. His estate had been hit by late spring frosts three times in the previous decade, and each time, the pattern had been identical: a warm March fooled the vines into waking early, tender green buds swelled along the neatly pruned spurs, and then a single clear April night dropped the temperature below freezing and turned an entire vintage into black mush. [SLOW DOWN] Henri was forty-eight years old, responsible for fourteen families whose livelihoods depended on that slope, and his bank credit was already stretched thin from two hail-damaged seasons. [PAUSE 1.5s] If he lost this crop, the estate would be sold off in pieces.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By the first week of April, the whispers in the village caf\u00e9 had hardened into open ridicule. [SPEED UP] The estate\u2019s managing director, a brisk, silver-haired man named Claude Moreau, drove his Peugeot up the gravel track and slammed the door before the dust had settled. \"Henri, the buyers from Beaune are driving past this hill every morning,\" Claude said, gesturing at the shaggy rows. \"They think we\u2019ve run out of money to pay the pruners. Cut them down today.\" [BEAT] Henri didn\u2019t reach for his secateurs. He pointed to the tips of the long, uncut canes, where tiny, fuzzy buds were already beginning to swell in the pale sun. \"Look at the top,\" Henri said quietly. \"And now look at the base, down by the trunk.\" Claude squinted. Down near the old wood, the basal buds\u2014the ones that would actually produce the year\u2019s fruit\u2014were still tightly shut, wrapped in brown scales like sleeping beetles. \"It\u2019s biology, Claude,\" Henri explained. \"In a vine, the highest buds wake up first. They pull all the sap, all the hormones, upward. As long as those top buds are alive, they keep the bottom buds asleep.\" [LOWER VOICE] Claude\u2019s jaw tightened. \"And if a frost doesn\u2019t come? You\u2019ll be pruning in May with the sap already running, bleeding the vines dry.\" \"Then I\u2019ll prune fast,\" Henri replied. Claude shook his head and walked back to his car. \"You have until Friday, Henri. After that, I\u2019m hiring a crew myself.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] That Thursday night, the sky cleared completely. [PAUSE 1.5s] By midnight, the wind died, and a heavy, crystalline stillness settled over the valley. Henri sat at his kitchen table with a mug of cold chicory, staring at the barograph needle as it crept steadily downward. Outside, the stars looked sharp enough to cut glass. He knew what the village thought of him\u2014that he was stubborn, that he was losing his nerve, that he had let his pride turn his vineyard into an eyesore. [LEAN IN] Around two in the morning, a terrible doubt crept into the room with the chill. What if the frost came harder than he calculated? What if it didn\u2019t just burn the top canes, but drove straight down into the trunk and killed the dormant buds too? [BEAT] Then he wouldn\u2019t just have lost the crop; he would have lost it while looking like a fool who hadn\u2019t even bothered to do the basic work of winter. At 3:45 a.m., the thermometer outside his window hit minus four degrees Celsius. [PAUSE 2s] Henri pulled on his coat, grabbed a flashlight, and walked out into the dark. The grass crunched like broken glass under his boots. Along the neighboring parcels, smudge pots were flaring orange in the blackness, men shouting as they dragged diesel heaters between the tidy rows. Henri had no smudge pots. He stood alone in [EMPHASIS: the ugliest vineyard in Meursault], watching frost crystals form on the wild, uncut canes above his head.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At dawn, the sun rose over the Jura mountains and hit the hillside like a floodlight. [STILLNESS] Henri walked to the boundary wall where Claude Moreau was already standing, his face pale in the morning light. Neither man spoke at first. Across the road, in the manicured, textbook-perfect vineyards, the tender green shoots that had been coaxed out by March warmth hung limp and blackened, smelling faintly of boiled spinach. Ninety percent of the primary buds were dead. [BEAT] Claude walked slowly into Henri\u2019s parcel, his polished shoes white with rime. He reached up and touched one of the high, uncut canes. The swollen top buds snapped off between his fingers, frozen solid and black at the core. \"You see?\" Claude whispered, his voice hollow. \"It took yours too.\" [LOWER VOICE] Henri stepped past him, knelt in the frozen grass beside the gnarled trunk, and pulled his secateurs from his holster. With two quick snips, he cut away the dead, sacrificial cane\u2014and peeled back the brown scales of a basal bud sitting six inches above the ground. [PAUSE 2s] Inside, the tissue was [EMPHASIS: bright, vivid, living green]. [WARM SMILE] \"No, Claude,\" Henri said softly. [EMPHASIS: \"The frost had taken the bait.\"] By leaving the vines unpruned, he had used [EMPHASIS: apical dominance] as a shield: the high buds had sacrificed themselves to the cold, keeping the true harvest locked safely in dormancy below. Claude stared at the green bud in the palm of Henri\u2019s hand, then looked back across the valley at the miles of neatly pruned, ruined rows. \"How long do we have to cut the rest?\" Claude asked. \"Ten days,\" Henri said, standing up and brushing the frost from his knees. \"Before the sap wakes the bottom.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] For the next week, Henri\u2019s crew worked from sunrise to dusk, stripping away the dead upper canes so the sleeping basal buds could finally drink the spring sun. [BEAT] In September, while neighboring estates harvested a fraction of their normal yield, Henri Delacroix\u2019s parcel produced a full, balanced crop of Chardonnay with crisp acidity and deep concentration. Word spread through Beaune, then across France, and within a decade, late pruning\u2014once dismissed as sheer laziness\u2014became a standard, respected defense against erratic spring weather. [PAUSE 1.5s] Henri never put up a sign, and he never bragged in the caf\u00e9. He simply kept the same rule nailed above his workbench: [EMPHASIS: neatness is not the same thing as readiness]. [PAUSE 2s]",
