      "annotatedScript": "[SLOW DOWN] In the third week of August 1961, the Bitterroot Mountains of western Montana were so dry that a boot heel striking granite could throw a spark. [BEAT] Martin Callahan, thirty-eight years old and eleven seasons into leading the Bitterroot Hotshots, stood at the base of Rattlesnake Ridge with nineteen men behind him and a wall of white smoke rising two miles to the south. [PAUSE 1.5s] His canvas pack weighed thirty-four pounds. His Pulaski axe hung from his right hand, its steel head nicked from three days of cutting line in lodgepole pine. Around him, the air tasted like copper and pitch. [SCENE SHIFT] At two-fifteen in the afternoon, the fire was supposed to be a routine mop-up. A lightning strike had chewed through four hundred acres of deadfall in the lower basin, and the Forest Service dispatch had sent Callahan's crew up the east flank to tie a cold trail into the creek bed. [LEAN IN] Martin knew the canyon. He knew how afternoon thermals pulled heat up the chutes like a chimney, and he knew that a crew boss's first job was never the fire itself. It was watching the wind. [EMPHASIS: watching the wind] Every ten minutes, he wet his thumb, held it above his collar, and watched the dust drift. At two-forty, the dust stopped drifting north. It hung dead in the air for four seconds, then snapped back toward the ridge. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SPEED UP] \"Packs on!\" Callahan barked, his voice cutting across the clink of canteens. \"We're pulling off the flank. Move up the spine!\" [BEAT] Beside him, his second-in-command, Ray Delgado, squinted down into the timber. Ray was twenty-six, built like a fence post, and had run three marathons before he ever touched a Pulaski. \"Boss, the creek is two hundred yards down,\" Ray called out, pointing with his shovel. \"We can wet down at the water.\" Callahan didn't look back. \"The creek is a bowl, Ray. If that crown fire jumps the saddle, the bowl turns into an oven. Up the ridge. Now.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] They climbed. For twenty minutes, the crew moved in single file through knee-high cheatgrass and scattered ponderosa. The grade was thirty-five degrees, loose shale sliding under Vibram soles. Halfway up, the sound changed. [LOWER VOICE] It wasn't the crackle of brush anymore. It was a low, steady roar, like a freight train coming through a tunnel. [BEAT] A spot fire had jumped the lower draw, and the afternoon wind was feeding it straight up the chute behind them. Ray looked over his shoulder and swore. \"It's closing at thirty yards a minute!\" he shouted. \"Tell them to drop the saws!\" Callahan checked his watch: three-oh-four. The summit was still four hundred yards above them, and the grass ahead was waist-high, cured yellow by six weeks without rain. \"Keep your tools!\" Callahan yelled back. \"If we hit rock, we dig!\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By three-twelve, the smoke had swallowed the sun. [SLOW DOWN] The light turned the color of an old bruise\u2014dirty amber, thick with floating ash the size of quarters. [BEAT] Men were coughing into their bandanas, lungs burning, legs cramping on the shale. Three hundred yards below, the wall of flame topped the first bench, sixty feet high, crowning from pine to pine with a sound that vibrated in their teeth. [LEAN IN] That was when the panic arrived. Not loud, not screaming\u2014just the sudden, contagious certainty that the ridge was too far. A rookie named Miller stumbled, his heavy chainsaw catching in a crevice. He unbuckled his pack and let it slide down the slope. Two other men saw him do it and dropped their Pulaskis. \"Leave it!\" Ray screamed, his eyes wide behind soot-streaked goggles. \"Run for the top! We can outrun it!\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Callahan grabbed Ray by the strap of his shirt and pulled him to a dead stop. Every instinct in Callahan's body\u2014eleven years of training, every rule in the Forest Service manual\u2014said the same thing: get to the high ground, get to bare rock, never stop moving uphill. [STILLNESS] Yet as he looked up at the final two hundred yards of dry cheatgrass, he did the math in his head. The crew was moving at three miles an hour. The fire, riding a forty-mile-an-hour thermal draft, was moving at twelve. [EMPHASIS: moving at twelve] They were not going to reach the top. Not even close.\n\n[LOWER VOICE] Callahan reached into his shirt pocket and pulled out a cardboard book of safety matches. [PAUSE 2s] He didn't explain. There was no time for a speech. He knelt in the waist-high yellow grass thirty feet ahead of his crew, struck a match against the strip, and dropped it at his boots. [BEAT] The grass caught with a dry *whoosh*. Within ten seconds, a crescent of orange flame began eating uphill, away from them, leaving a widening circle of smoking, blackened earth behind it. Ray stared at him as if he had lost his mind. \"Callahan! What the hell are you doing? You're lighting a fire in front of us!\" [SPEED UP] \"Get in the black!\" Callahan roared, his voice cracking over the approaching thunder. \"Step into the ashes! Lie flat!\" [BEAT] Nobody moved. Nineteen men stood frozen between two fires\u2014the towering inferno roaring up from the canyon, and the small, erratic ring of smoke their own boss had just lit. \"I'm not dying in your bonfire!\" Ray shouted, backing away toward the summit. \"Come on!\" Five men bolted after Ray, scrambling blindly up the shale toward the ridge. [PAUSE 1.5s] Callahan didn't chase them. He dropped to his knees in the hot, smoking dirt of his own little burn, pulled his wet bandana over his nose, and waved the remaining fourteen men down beside him. \"Face in the dirt!\" he ordered. \"Don't look up!\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [STILLNESS] At three-seventeen, the main fire arrived. [LOWER VOICE] It did not pass around them. It roared directly over the blackened circle where Martin Callahan and fourteen men lay pressed against the earth, their knuckles white around their Pulaskis, their mouths pressed into the ash. [PAUSE 2s] For forty-five seconds, the air above them was a solid ceiling of orange heat. The wind sucked the oxygen out of their lungs. Sparks rained down on their canvas jackets, hissing where sweat met wool. [BEAT] And then, just as suddenly, the roar moved past. [SLOW DOWN] Because Callahan's small fire had already burned the cheatgrass inside that twenty-foot circle, [EMPHASIS: the fire had nothing left to eat]. It split around the black, surged up the remaining two hundred yards of the ridge, and consumed the summit in less than a minute. [PAUSE 1.5s] When Callahan finally lifted his head, the sky was gray and quiet. All fourteen men beside him were alive, coughing, blistered, and covered in soot, but breathing. Up on the ridge, where Ray Delgado and the five fastest runners had sprinted for safety, the grass had burned to white powder. [WARM SMILE] Two hours later, when the rescue chopper set down on the cold black, the pilot asked Callahan how he knew to stop running when every nerve in his body told him to climb. Callahan looked back down the charred slope, wiped the ash from his goggles, and said, \"I didn't outrun it. I just took away what it came for.\"",
