      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] In November of 1962, the fog over Puget Sound did not roll in from the water. [PAUSE 1.5s] [EMPHASIS: It simply erased the world.] One moment, Captain Arthur Lindqvist could see the red crane arms along the Duwamish Waterway, the wet gleam of the pilings, and the distant yellow porch lights of West Seattle climbing the bluff. Five minutes later, at four-fifteen in the afternoon, the glass of the wheelhouse on the tugboat *Kestrel* turned the color of wet wool. [BEAT] Arthur was fifty-eight years old, with knuckles swollen from thirty winters of hemp hawser and a wool cap pulled low over ears that could still tell the difference between a ferry's diesel thrum and a freighter's deeper pulse. Behind the *Kestrel*, on a hundred and eighty feet of braided steel cable, rode a flat-deck barge stacked twelve feet high with Douglas fir timber bound for Tacoma. [SLOW DOWN] That was the easy part. The hard part was standing three feet to his left and four feet behind him: Tommy Reyes, twenty-one years old, six weeks out of high school in Yakima, and Owen Gallagher, nineteen, whose entire maritime career consisted of two sunny afternoons painting a dock in Ballard. [PAUSE 1.5s] Neither boy had ever stood watch in a true Puget Sound soup, the kind that muffles a shout at ten paces and turns every deck light into a blinding white halo. Arthur checked the brass barometer\u2014twenty-nine point four and dropping\u2014and felt the cold knot settle behind his ribs. [LOWER VOICE] The tide was ebbing fast, pulling them toward the narrow rock-lined throat of Harbor Island, where three channels met and a single misjudged turn meant sixty tons of timber slamming into a concrete pier.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At four-thirty, Arthur did what the harbor manual, the Coast Guard, and thirty years of habit told him to do. He reached up and pulled the brass lanyard of the *Kestrel*'s air horn. [EMPHASIS: The blast shook the wheelhouse glass.] \"Sixty seconds,\" Arthur told Tommy, nodding toward the stopwatch clipped to the chart table. \"Every minute on the minute, you pull that cord. Owen, you stay on the bow with the bell. You listen for anything answering back.\" [SPEED UP] For the first twenty minutes, the routine felt like armor. The horn bellowed\u2014*braaaam*\u2014and the sound rolled out into the gray wall, then slapped back off the corrugated tin warehouses along the east bank, delayed by two seconds, distorted into a ghostly double echo. \"I hear another tug!\" Owen yelled from the bow, his voice cracking over the intercom. \"Port side! Close!\" [BEAT] Arthur's hand tightened on the spoked wooden wheel. He squinted at the small green radar screen mounted beside the compass, cranking the gain knob clockwise until the phosphor glowed bright emerald. Instead of a clean picture, the screen erupted in a blizzard of speckles\u2014rain, chop, and the high steel superstructure of the timber barge behind them bouncing false returns across the glass. \"That's not a tug, Owen,\" Arthur called back, trying to keep his voice flat. \"That's our own horn bouncing off the grain elevator.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] \"Are you sure, Cap?\" Tommy asked, his hand trembling slightly on the lanyard. \"Because it sounded right off our beam.\" Arthur didn't answer. He couldn't. On the radar, three separate bright blobs were drifting across the center line, and he had no idea which one was a buoy, which one was a moored scow, and which one was a ghost.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] By five-fifteen, the *Kestrel* was creeping at four knots, blind as a mole, and the wheelhouse smelled of wet wool, diesel, and cold sweat. Arthur's eyes burned from staring into the green snow of the radar. Every sixty seconds, Tommy dutifully yanked the lanyard, and every sixty seconds the air horn tore the quiet apart, leaving their ears ringing for the next forty. [BEAT] Twice, Owen shouted from the bow that he heard a bell, only to retract it ten seconds later when the echo died. The ebb tide was pushing their stern sideways now; Arthur could feel it in the heavy, sluggish resistance of the rudder. If they drifted thirty yards too far west, they would kiss the submerged riprap off Duwamish Head. If they stayed too far east, they risked clipping the anchored barges waiting for the morning shift. [LEAN IN] \"Cap,\" Tommy said, his voice tight. \"It's been sixty seconds. Should I pull it?\" Arthur looked at the young man's white knuckles, then at the radar screen where the green clutter had fused into a solid, useless smear. [EMPHASIS: Too much signal looks identical to blindness.] Every tool on the boat\u2014the horn, the radar, the deck floodlights reflecting off the mist\u2014was working at full capacity, and together they had built a cage of noise around the wheelhouse. Arthur's chest ached with the quiet dread every captain knows: the moment you realize that following the book is about to put you on the rocks.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [STILLNESS] \"No,\" Arthur said. Tommy froze, his hand half-raised to the lanyard. \"Cap? The rule\u2014\" \"Let go of the cord, Tommy.\" [PAUSE 2s] Arthur reached over and clicked the *Kestrel*'s throttle back to dead slow, then eased the clutch into neutral so the tug's heavy diesel dropped from a roar to a low, rhythmic mutter. Next, he turned to the radar\u2014not to shut it off, but to do the one thing no manual ever suggested. He gripped the gain knob and [EMPHASIS: turned the gain down]\u2014counter-clockwise, stripping away the bright green blizzard until the screen went almost completely dark, leaving only the faintest, barest ghost of a sweep. \"What are you doing?\" Tommy whispered. \"If we don't blow the horn, nobody knows we're here.\" \"Nobody knows where we are anyway,\" Arthur said quietly. \"They're just hearing echoes. Now shut the wheelhouse door.\" [LOWER VOICE] For [EMPHASIS: a full ninety seconds of nothing], the *Kestrel* drifted in the gray dark. No horn. No floodlights. No shouting over the intercom. [PAUSE 2s] At first, there was only the slap of black water against the steel hull. Then, at forty seconds, Owen's voice came through the open side window\u2014not yelling, just speaking. \"Cap...\" he said. \"There's a bell. Three-second interval. Dead ahead, maybe two hundred yards.\" Arthur leaned out into the damp chill. Beneath the vanished roar of his own horn, he heard it: the faint, unmistakable *clang-clang* of the Harbor Island channel buoy, followed a moment later by the low, steady chug of a cannery tender idling somewhere off their starboard quarter\u2014a sound that had been completely buried under their own sixty-second blasts. [WARM SMILE] And when Arthur glanced back at the darkened radar screen, a single, sharp pinprick of green light sat cleanly on the three-o'clock line, stripped of every false echo.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Arthur didn't touch the horn again until they were clear of the throat. He nudged the *Kestrel* back into gear, using the faint buoy bell and the single clean dot on the dimmed screen to thread the *Kestrel* and its sixty tons of Douglas fir past the riprap with forty yards to spare. At six-ten, the fog thinned just enough for the amber sodium lights of the Tacoma waterway to bleed through the mist, and the barge slid neatly into its berth. [BEAT] Tommy stood by the chart table, coiling the lanyard he hadn't touched in forty minutes. \"I thought the horn was supposed to keep us safe,\" he said. Arthur pulled off his wet wool cap and rubbed his eyes. \"It does,\" he said, \"when you're in open water. But in a narrow channel, all you're doing is shouting at the walls and calling the echo another ship.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] [LOWER VOICE] The two boys never forgot that night, and neither did the harbor pilots who heard the story over coffee at the Pier 54 diner. [EMPHASIS: Silence was not the absence of information.] It was the only way to clear the room so the truth could finally be heard.",
