      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] In November of 1987, Father Thomas Gallagher kept a pair of rubber-soled shoes under his desk at St. Jude's Mercy Hospital in Pittsburgh because the linoleum on the third floor was always damp. [SLOW DOWN] Outside, sleet ticked against the wire-reinforced glass of the stairwell. Inside, the air smelled of floor wax, boiled cabbage from the evening tray cart, and the sharp, metallic tang of radiator heat. [BEAT] Gallagher was fifty-eight years old, a hospital chaplain with silver-rimmed glasses and a habit of carrying a small, cloth-bound notebook in his breast pocket. His job, as he understood it, was to sit with people in the hours when certainty ran out. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nAt ten minutes past nine that Tuesday, the charge nurse, a brisk woman named Evelyn Marsh, caught him by the elbow near the nurses' station. She nodded toward Waiting Room B, a narrow alcove at the end of the surgical wing with four vinyl chairs, a vending machine that hummed like a tired refrigerator, and a single framed print of a sailboat. [LOWER VOICE] \"Arthur Halvorsen is in recovery,\" Evelyn said, keeping her voice low. \"His heart held, thank God. But his two sons are in there, Thomas. They haven't spoken since 1965. If you don't get between them, security will.\" [BEAT]\n\nGallagher knew the Halvorsen name from the intake chart. Arthur was eighty-one, a retired steelworker from Homestead. His sons were Daniel, sixty, a foreman at a foundry in Butler, and Raymond, fifty-six, a high-school band director who had moved to Ohio thirty years earlier. [EMPHASIS: twenty-two years of practiced silence] stood between them, built out of an old argument over a family house, a missed funeral, and the stubborn pride of men who believed that apologizing meant losing. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Gallagher pushed open the door to Waiting Room B. Daniel sat in the corner nearest the window, his heavy wool coat still buttoned to the throat, a thermos balanced between his boots. Raymond sat across from him by the vending machine, his knees angled toward the hallway as if he were ready to bolt. Neither man looked up. [BEAT] \"Gentlemen,\" Gallagher said gently, taking the empty chair between them. \"Nurse Marsh tells me your father came through surgery well. The doctor will be out in about forty minutes.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nDaniel stared at the sailboat print. \"Good,\" he said, the word flat as a dropped wrench. Raymond nodded once toward the floor. \"Tell the doctor I'm glad,\" Raymond said, speaking to Gallagher as though Daniel were a piece of furniture. [SPEED UP] For the next twenty minutes, Gallagher tried every tool he had learned in thirty years of ministry. He asked about their drive in through the sleet. He mentioned Arthur's strength. He offered a quiet prayer. [BEAT] Every bridge he built, they burned before he finished the sentence. When he asked Daniel if he wanted coffee, Daniel said, \"I brought my own.\" When he asked Raymond if he had a long drive back, Raymond said, \"Not long enough.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nFinally, Gallagher leaned forward and tried the direct approach. [LEAN IN] \"Your father is eighty-one years old,\" he said. \"He asked for both of you before they wheeled him in. Whatever happened between you two, tonight is not the night to carry it.\" [STILLNESS] Daniel's jaw tightened. He turned his head just enough to look at the chaplain, not at his brother. \"Father, with respect, you don't know what he did.\" Raymond's eyes snapped up. \"What I did? You're the one who changed the locks before the ink was dry!\" [PAUSE 1.5s] The room flared hot in an instant. Both men stood half-way out of their vinyl chairs, their voices rising over the hum of the vending machine. Gallagher raised both hands, stepping between them, and the silence that followed was worse than the shouting\u2014cold, brittle, and final. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] At ten-fifteen, Gallagher walked out into the corridor to fetch a cup of water, his shoulders aching. He leaned against the cool cinderblock wall outside the nurses' station and rubbed the bridge of his nose. He had failed. Worse, he had made it harder. By stepping into the middle of the room and trying to force a truce, he had given both brothers a stage on which to perform their grievance. [BEAT] He looked back down the hall. Through the narrow glass pane in the door of Waiting Room B, he could see the backs of their heads\u2014two dark silhouettes, angled away from each other, waiting out the clock. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nThen Evelyn Marsh hurried past with a clipboard. \"Thomas,\" she said, \"room 314 just called. Mrs. Kowalski's family is asking for you. Her daughter's flight got delayed in Chicago, and she's panicking.\" Gallagher hesitated. He glanced back at Waiting Room B. If he left now, Daniel and Raymond would sit in that tiny room alone until the surgeon arrived. They might argue again. One of them might even walk out before Arthur woke up. [LOWER VOICE] \"I'll be right there,\" Gallagher told Evelyn. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] He walked back into Waiting Room B, not to preach, and not to mediate. He picked up his own worn canvas coat from the fourth chair, but as he reached the door, his pager buzzed sharply on his belt. In his hurry to silence it and grab his notebook, he draped his coat over the back of the chair nearest the radiator\u2014right between Daniel's boots and Raymond's knees\u2014and stepped out into the hall. [BEAT] \"Gentlemen,\" he said from the doorway, \"I have an emergency down the hall. I'll be back before the doctor arrives.\" He let the door swing shut. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Down in room 314, Gallagher spent twenty-five minutes calming Mrs. Kowalski's daughter over the phone. When he finally walked back toward Waiting Room B at ten-forty-five, the sleet had turned to a steady, drumming rain against the hallway windows. He braced himself for the worst\u2014an empty room, or an angry nurse. [STILLNESS] Instead, when he pushed the door open, he stopped on the threshold. [PAUSE 2s]\n\nThe radiator had clicked on high while he was gone, hissing steam into the small room. Daniel's heavy wool coat was off now, folded neatly across his lap. Raymond had moved one chair closer to the window to get out of the draft from the hallway door. [LOWER VOICE] And between them, on the empty vinyl chair, Gallagher's own canvas coat was folded in half, with Daniel's thermos resting on one corner of the seat and Raymond's car keys resting on the other. [BEAT] Neither man was smiling. They were not hugging. But Daniel was speaking, his voice low and rough, explaining to Raymond how the old furnace in their father's house had finally given out last winter. Raymond was listening, nodding slowly, his hands clasped between his knees. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nGallagher cleared his throat softly. Both men looked up. \"The surgeon just stepped off the elevator,\" Gallagher said. [WARM SMILE] \"Your father's going to be fine.\" Daniel reached down, picked up his thermos, and handed the chaplain's canvas coat back to him. [EMPHASIS: the coat was still warm] from the radiator. \"You forgot this, Father,\" Daniel said. \"It was getting in the way.\" Raymond stood up, slipped his keys into his pocket, and looked at his brother. \"Come on, Dan,\" Raymond said quietly. \"Let's go see Dad.\" [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Years later, when Gallagher taught younger chaplains at the diocese, he never told them to memorize better arguments for forgiveness. [SLOW DOWN] He told them about Waiting Room B. He told them that when two people are locked in a stalemate, the mediator's presence can accidentally become the excuse neither side has to move. [EMPHASIS: a problem neither man could solve alone]\u2014even something as small as a damp coat on a warm radiator\u2014sometimes requires stepping out of the room so the people inside can finally remember how to share the space. [PAUSE 2s]",
